<<~ ahu slot=`#/handback-e2e-shard-3-4`>>

!! Handback — E2E shard 3/4

''UNKNOWN.'' Both logs emit the same diagnostic from `tests/e2e/boot-holds-the-store.test.ts`, so this appears to be the same test as the first run:

> `boot-holds-the-store acts {"besideTheAct":"error:{\"code\":\"error\",\"message\":\"the store at … stays held by a direct lares command … for one act — run ","atClaim":"daemon","atBoot":"daemon","begun":{"atClaim":"claimed","atBoot":"boot"}}`

That is the test’s diagnostic, not its failed assertion. The logs use blob/JSON reporters and do not print the assertion or error that made Vitest exit 1; the report itself is not among the supplied files. I can’t establish whether this was the only failed test or name the first failed assertion. The first triage had the same gap, so two reds do not support calling it a flake.

The founder-standing changes look ''unlikely to explain a store-ownership failure''. The daemon’s founder dyad read in `operator-daemon-behavior.ts` is inside the face-join action; the seat’s `founderStanding` callback is used when processing face-join grants. This test creates and boots a vessel, then probes its store rendezvous; it does not issue a face join. A pending or refused grant could affect seating, but the supplied trace does not connect that path to the test’s store-ownership assertions.

I found no known-flake note for this test under `tools/`. The shard weights give it weight ''97''; it is in the weighted `tests` shard set, not identified as a heavy-roster test. The available CI evidence does not show a green shard-3 run for this span: `e723dda17` is the base/test-door commit, not the lane head.

''Next measure:'' have the lead run `tests/e2e/boot-holds-the-store.test.ts` at `5c3d032dc` and `5ba18c736`, each with a 600-second cap, and preserve the JSON report so the first failed assertion is visible. This test opens sockets and spawns a daemon, so it is ''lead runs'' under the seat law.

<<~/ahu>>

